From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: prospero-win <prospero-win@invalid>
Date: Sun, 16 Nov 2026 13:00:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] server: Sample the thread the wait watchdog says is wedged.

The watchdog already finds it: a thread in no wait that has asked the
server for nothing for over a second. What it cannot do is say where that
thread is. dump_guest_stack() reads the WoW64 CPU area and requires
IMAGE_FILE_MACHINE_I386, so on a 64-bit title it prints nothing at all,
which is why Hollow Knight's main thread -- silent for 90 seconds with
every other thread parked on a semaphore -- has never had a stack.

The glue's SIGALRM sampler can print rip/rsp and a bounded stack for a
64-bit thread. It just never lands on the right one: ITIMER_REAL is
process-directed, and across 473 samples the kernel chose threads sitting
in waits every time. The server knows exactly which thread is wedged and
runs in the same process, so it can signal that one.

Guarded by PW_PS4_GUEST_SAMPLER=1, the same switch that installs the
handler: without it nothing handles SIGALRM and the default action would
end the game. Bounded to eight pokes, because a wedge does not end.
---
 server/thread.c | 46 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 46 insertions(+)

diff --git a/server/thread.c b/server/thread.c
index 1111111..2222222 100644
--- a/server/thread.c
+++ b/server/thread.c
@@ -1191,6 +1191,49 @@
     }
 }
 
+#ifdef __PROSPERO__
+/* The console has no thr_kill2 of its own: the wineserver PRX's platform shim maps the id
+ * ntdll registered for each thread to its pthread_t and calls pthread_kill (ptrace.c). */
+extern int thr_kill2( pid_t pid, long id, int sig );
+
+/* A thread in no wait that has asked the server for nothing for over a second is wedged in
+ * host code, and dump_guest_stack() cannot say where: it reads the WoW64 CPU area, which
+ * only a 32-bit guest has, so on a 64-bit title every such thread has been invisible.
+ * The glue's SIGALRM sampler does print rip/rsp and a bounded stack, but ITIMER_REAL is
+ * process-directed and the kernel kept delivering to threads parked in waits -- 473 samples
+ * on Hollow Knight, not one of them the wedged thread. Send the signal to this exact
+ * thread; the handler the sampler already installed then runs on it.
+ *
+ * Once per thread, not a global budget: Hollow Knight has 32 threads outside any wait, and
+ * a budget of eight was spent on Unity's workers before the main thread -- the only one
+ * that matters -- was ever reached. Remembering ids costs nothing and samples each once.
+ *
+ * Only with PW_PS4_GUEST_SAMPLER=1: without the sampler nothing handles SIGALRM and the
+ * default action would end the game. */
+static void ps5_poke_silent_thread( struct thread *thread )
+{
+    static unsigned int poked[512];
+    static unsigned int npoked;
+    static int armed = -1;
+    unsigned int i;
+
+    if (armed < 0)
+    {
+        const char *text = getenv( "PW_PS4_GUEST_SAMPLER" );
+        armed = text && text[0] == '1';
+    }
+    if (!armed || thread->unix_pid == -1 || thread->unix_tid == -1) return;
+    for (i = 0; i < npoked; i++) if (poked[i] == thread->id) return;
+    if (npoked >= sizeof(poked) / sizeof(poked[0])) return;
+    poked[npoked++] = thread->id;
+    fprintf( stderr, "wine-ps5: poke silent tid=%04x unix_tid=%d n=%u rc=%d\n",
+             thread->id, thread->unix_tid, npoked,
+             thr_kill2( thread->unix_pid, thread->unix_tid, SIGALRM ) );
+}
+#else
+static void ps5_poke_silent_thread( struct thread *thread ) { (void)thread; }
+#endif
+
 static void wait_watchdog( void *arg )
 {
     struct thread *thread;
@@ -1207,7 +1250,10 @@
          * from the WoW64 CPU area (TEB64 TlsSlots[WOW64_TLS_CPURESERVED]).
          * The server runs in the game's process, so it can read it. */
         if (!thread->wait && thread->teb && monotonic_time - thread->ps5_last_req_time > TICKS_PER_SEC)
+        {
             dump_guest_stack( thread, 32, 128 );
+            ps5_poke_silent_thread( thread );
+        }
         /* In one wait for over three seconds: its i386 stack once per wait,
          * deeper, so that the frames and the critical sections held further
          * up (a DllMain under the loader lock) show. */
